  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi></math> and <math> <mi>&#955;</mi> </math> be positive real numbers. Suppose <math> <mi>P</mi> </math> is an end point of the latus rectum of the parabola <math> <msup> <mi>y</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> <mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>x</mi> </math>, and suppose the ellipse <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <msup> <mi>x</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mrow> <msup> <mi>a</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>+</mo><mfrac> <mrow> <msup> <mi>y</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> <mrow> <msup> <mi>b</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow> </math> passes through the point <math> <mi>P</mi> </math>. If the tangents to the parabola and the ellipse at the point <math> <mi>P</mi> </math> are perpendicular to each other, then the eccentricity of the ellipse is",
    "image": null
